## brief_artifacts
[{"brief_id":"brief-92dcf66c-1a9c-4885-a6af-7f57b149a3d8","artifact_kind":"researcher","publication_kind":"research","revision_id":"289a1fe6-8b7b-4e8a-9c4d-22db82ba54d0","path":"/Users/normy/autobyteus_org/autobyteus-worktrees/universal-application-framework-proposal-analysis/applications/brief-studio/.autobyteus/dev/data/applications/bundle-app__7374616e64616c6f6e65__62726965662d73747564696f/runtime/brief-studio/research.md","description":"Concise research checkpoint for the API graph publication shutdown brief.","body":"# API REV 008 graph publication shutdown 1785398337317 — Research Checkpoint\n\n## Key findings\n- The brief should define a controlled shutdown of graph publication, separating the public-facing publish path from graph construction, reads, and already-committed artifacts.\n- A safe sequence is: announce freeze, stop new publication intake, drain or explicitly cancel in-flight jobs, verify queue and worker state, then disable publishing and retain read-only access where possible.\n- Idempotent shutdown controls, an auditable operator action, and a clear status endpoint are essential to prevent duplicate publishes, partial graph versions, or ambiguous “stopped” states.\n- Recovery should preserve the last known-good graph snapshot and support a reversible restart with replay/reconciliation of work accepted before the cutoff.\n\n## Risks / open questions\n- The exact publication topology, queue semantics, and ownership of in-flight jobs are not yet specified; drain guarantees may differ by implementation.\n- Consumers may treat publication success as availability of the new graph, creating consistency or cache-invalidation gaps during shutdown.\n- Required RTO/RPO, maintenance window, notification audience, and authorization/audit requirements need confirmation before rollout.\n\n## Recommendations / next steps\n- Define a shutdown state machine with explicit cut-off time, drain timeout, cancellation policy, health signals, and operator permissions.\n- Add preflight/postflight checks for queue depth, worker leases, snapshot integrity, downstream notifications, and read-path health.\n- Make the runbook reversible: capture a checkpoint, publish a final status event, verify the retained snapshot, and document restart/replay validation.\n","producer_member_route_key":"researcher","updated_at":"2026-07-30T07:59:40.295Z"},
